# Half of Slovakia's new-build supply is in Bratislava, and prices outside it are a third lower

The Slovak new-build market consists of {totalAvailable} available flats in {totalProjects} projects across {towns} towns. Half the supply is in Bratislava. The median asking price is {baScopeMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in Bratislava and {restMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in the rest of the country. The median flat price is {baScopePrice} € in Bratislava and {restPrice} € in the other towns.

As of {asOfEn} the supply on the Slovak new-build market was {totalAvailable} available flats across {totalProjects} projects in {towns} towns. Bratislava holds {baAvailable} of them, or {baSharePctEn}% of the whole supply; the remaining {restAvailable} are spread across {restTowns} towns. The largest regional markets are Nitra with {nrAvailable} flats, Trnava with {ttAvailable}, Košice with {keAvailable}, Prešov with {poAvailable} and Poprad with {ppAvailable}.

The median asking price of these flats reached {baScopeMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in Bratislava and {restMedian} €/m² incl. VAT in the rest of the country, or {restBelowBaPctEn}% less. After Bratislava the dearest are Košice at {keM2} €/m² incl. VAT, Žilina at {zaM2} and Banská Bystrica at {bbM2}, followed by Trnava ({ttM2}), Trenčín ({tnM2}) and Nitra ({nrM2}). The lowest unit price among the towns with a larger supply is {cheapestM2Town} at {lcM2} €/m² incl. VAT.

The median absolute price of a flat on offer was {baScopePrice} € incl. VAT in Bratislava at {baScopeAreaEn} m², and {restPrice} € in the other towns at {restAreaEn} m².

